t 6 & =3\\cdot (C-2) \\\\ 6C-24 & =3C-6 \\\\ 6C-24-3C & =3C-6-3C \\\\ 3C-24 & =-6 \\\\ 3C-24+24 & =-6+24 \\\\ 3C & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3C}{3} & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio of the width of a volleyball court to its length is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ If the length of the volleyball court is $18$ meters, what is its width?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14d57c7d-ff6e-530b-b67b-f43cfc293439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551238 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of width to length is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the width and the denominator represents the length.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $18$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 9}{2\\times 9}=\\frac{9}{18}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the volleyball court is $9$ meters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-318203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1071 "When a new app was first launched, it had $60$ downloads. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of downloads increased by $120%$ of the number of downloads at the end of the previous day.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $D$ is the estimated number of downloads $t$ days after the app was launched, and $t\\le 7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1818fb31-e6dd-51ed-98a5-48141bc40666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551241 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=60$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=60\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=60\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shows a plot of the line $x=-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26fb6f7e-8d80-56e2-95a0-d9d21d4fb0e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551249 #edb/ref 1074 "On the line $x=-1,$ every point has an $x$-coordinate of $-1.$\n\nSo, the plot of $x=-1$ is the following vertical line:\n\n![](images/00/00b0a9c54b56b91417cb89752b39b1d5969ca631cc56da84815a0bb5d4cbead3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}}$ is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d3bb2ee-cb7d-5b52-9b6d-3169a083b4de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551254 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}} & = \\\\ {({(4b)}^{2})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\frac{3t+6}{4t+8}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6410f1b3-233a-5db4-a768-91ae7f99b09a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551283 #edb/ref 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t+6}{4t+8} & =\\frac{3\\cdot (t+2)}{4\\cdot (t+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{(t+2)}}{4\\cdot \\cancel{(t+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{y}{3}\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66604494-d5d9-5eb8-81e3-a76e00656f7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551284 #edb/ref 1074 "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}3)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & \\ge -9 \\\\ y & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge -9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}$ is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "840068df-b5bb-5a8c-a125-569067cf40da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551299 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}={(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{9}) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1069 "q-357516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1071 "Convert $\\frac{253}{6}$ into a repeating decimal using long division." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c3882c6-2da8-5fcb-8c4a-eadb749133f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551315 #edb/ref 1074 "To write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n253÷6\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, continuing until a repeating pattern of digits appears after the decimal point:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<br/><br/><table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td al